    "why": "A SYMMETRIC PAIR THAT ACTUALLY SATURATES. T13 at 10 req/s and T15 at 20 req/s both answered the FAIRNESS question -- degradation spread 1.006 to 1.031, so MPS creates no winner when in-flight depth is equal -- and neither answered the CAPACITY question, because both stayed above 99.5% of their solo-sum throughput. T15 was commissioned to saturate and failed: doubling the rate roughly doubled delivered throughput (1,307 -> 2,589 tok/s), which is what not-yet-saturated looks like. So double it again. Two Qwen2.5-1.5B-AWQ instances have delivered 1,327 tok/s each; 40 req/s demands far more than the card can serve, which is the only way to learn whether the MPS throughput gain measured on UNLIKE pairs (T5 +6.4%, T14 +7.6%) also appears when the tenants are identical. Registered prediction: it does, and at a similar magnitude, because the gain comes from removing context-switch cost rather than from any asymmetry between the tenants. If it does NOT appear, then every throughput number in this study depends on the pair being unlike, which would be a significant and previously unsuspected caveat. Watch the host-RAM guard: two 1.5B tenants fit, but this tier is the most in-flight requests the node has held."
  },
